{% extends "layout.njk" %}
{% import "macros.njk" as m %}
{% set title = "Notice" %}
{% set slug = "notice" %}

{% block content %}
<h1>Notice</h1>
<p class="lede">
  The phone's toast. Notices open low, just above the dock, where the thumb and the eyes already are, and stack upward.
  How long each stays and showing one at a time come from @sparkstone/kit.
</p>

{{ m.alpha() }}

{{ m.classes([
  ["notices", "The stack, fixed above the dock (reads --dock-h)"],
  ["notice", "One message: an edged card on a tint"],
  ["notice-success", "Something worked"],
  ["notice-warning", "Something to be careful about"],
  ["notice-error", "Something went wrong"],
  ["notice-art", "The thing it's about, in a coin"],
  ["notice-text", "A bold line and an optional detail in <small>"],
  ["notice-actions", "Undo, Edit"],
  ["notice-link", "The whole notice is one link, on its text"],
  ["notice-go", "The › at the end of a link"]
]) }}

<h2>Basic</h2>
<p>The stack is shown in place here rather than fixed to the bottom.</p>
{% set classless %}
<ul class="notices" style="position: static; padding: 0">
  <li class="notice">
    <span class="notice-art" aria-hidden="true">🍼</span>
    <span class="notice-text"><strong>Logged bottle 90 ml</strong><small>Use by 12:11 PM</small></span>
    <span class="notice-actions"><button class="btn btn-sm">Undo</button></span>
  </li>
  <li class="notice notice-success">
    <span class="notice-text"><strong>Invite link copied</strong></span>
  </li>
  <li class="notice notice-warning">
    <span class="notice-text"><strong>Syncing…</strong><small>Your changes are saved on this phone.</small></span>
  </li>
  <li class="notice notice-error">
    <span class="notice-text"><strong>The server isn't answering.</strong><small>Your moves go through once it's back.</small></span>
  </li>
</ul>
{% endset %}
{{ m.demo("notice-basic", classless) }}

<h2>Link</h2>
<p>A notice that goes somewhere: the link sits on its text and covers the whole notice, with a › at the end. Buttons in it still take their own taps.</p>
{% set classless %}
<ul class="notices" style="position: static; padding: 0">
  <li class="notice notice-link">
    <span class="notice-text"><a href="#"><strong>Your turn in another game</strong></a></span>
    <span class="notice-go" aria-hidden="true">›</span>
  </li>
  <li class="notice notice-link">
    <span class="notice-art" aria-hidden="true">🏔️</span>
    <span class="notice-text"><a href="#"><strong>Sam reached the summit</strong></a><small>See the climb</small></span>
    <span class="notice-go" aria-hidden="true">›</span>
  </li>
</ul>
{% endset %}
{{ m.demo("notice-link", classless) }}

<h2>Fitting the app</h2>
<p>
  A notice takes the app's colours: its edge and tint are the accent, from <code>--primary</code>. Each notice is a
  re-theme boundary, so setting <code>--primary</code> on <code>.notice</code> recolours all of them, the same way as
  anywhere else in the library. Anything more, override from the app's own stylesheet: the library sits in a cascade
  layer, so the app's rules win.
</p>
{% set classless %}
<ul class="notices" style="position: static; padding: 0">
  <li class="notice" style="--primary: oklch(0.72 0.14 80)"><span class="notice-text"><strong>.notice { --primary: gold }</strong></span></li>
</ul>
{% endset %}
{{ m.demo("notice-styling", classless) }}
{% endblock %}
